WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.761
A workshop has a board

2
00:00:04.841 --> 00:00:07.430
eighty-four centimetres long and

3
00:00:07.511 --> 00:00:09.452
thirty centimetres wide.

4
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:12.041
It wants to cut the whole board

5
00:00:12.122 --> 00:00:15.439
into equal squares, as large as possible

6
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:17.380
with nothing left over.

7
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.226
How big can the squares be?

8
00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:21.890
Pause and decide.

9
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.776
Try squares of side five.

10
00:00:27.776 --> 00:00:31.927
Five divides thirty, so six rows fit across.

11
00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:34.233
But along the long edge

12
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:37.922
sixteen squares use eighty centimetres

13
00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:40.597
and a strip four centimetres

14
00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.350
wide is left over.

15
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.015
That is a division with remainder

16
00:00:46.015 --> 00:00:49.428
eighty-four is sixteen times five

17
00:00:49.428 --> 00:00:50.500
plus four.

18
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.536
Side twelve fails the other way.

19
00:00:53.536 --> 00:00:56.808
Seven squares fit along the long edge

20
00:00:56.808 --> 00:01:00.333
but thirty is two times twelve, plus six

21
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:03.690
so a strip six wide is left at the top.

22
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.390
A square fits only if its side

23
00:01:06.469 --> 00:01:08.114
divides both numbers.

24
00:01:08.114 --> 00:01:11.248
So we want the greatest common divisor.

25
00:01:11.326 --> 00:01:13.598
And a strip that is left over

26
00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:15.400
is always a remainder.

27
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.481
Division with remainder works for any

28
00:01:19.576 --> 00:01:22.320
integer a and any positive b.

29
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.874
Take twenty-six and seven.

30
00:01:24.874 --> 00:01:28.091
Jump from zero in steps of seven

31
00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:31.781
as long as you do not pass twenty-six.

32
00:01:31.781 --> 00:01:34.620
Three jumps reach twenty-one.

33
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.785
The piece left over

34
00:01:36.785 --> 00:01:40.415
from twenty-one to twenty-six, is five.

35
00:01:40.415 --> 00:01:43.682
So twenty-six is three times seven

36
00:01:43.682 --> 00:01:44.680
plus five.

37
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.584
The remainder is at least zero

38
00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:49.490
and less than seven.

39
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.530
The division theorem says such

40
00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:54.681
a q and r always exist

41
00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.640
and that there is only one pair.

42
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.208
Why does the remainder exist?

43
00:02:01.311 --> 00:02:04.196
Start at twenty-six and jump

44
00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:06.669
back in steps of seven

45
00:02:06.669 --> 00:02:11.202
staying at zero or above: nineteen, twelve

46
00:02:11.202 --> 00:02:11.820
five.

47
00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:15.617
The numbers a minus q times b that are not

48
00:02:15.699 --> 00:02:18.899
negative form a set of natural numbers.

49
00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:21.525
By the well-ordering principle

50
00:02:21.525 --> 00:02:23.987
this set has a least element.

51
00:02:23.987 --> 00:02:24.890
Call it r.

52
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.263
If r were seven or more

53
00:02:27.348 --> 00:02:29.708
we could jump back once more

54
00:02:29.793 --> 00:02:31.985
and stay at zero or above

55
00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.430
so r would not be the least.

56
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.200
So r is less than b.

57
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.426
Now uniqueness.

58
00:02:38.426 --> 00:02:42.924
Suppose two pairs both work: q b plus r

59
00:02:42.924 --> 00:02:45.997
and q prime b plus r prime.

60
00:02:45.997 --> 00:02:47.094
Subtract.

61
00:02:47.094 --> 00:02:49.947
Q minus q prime, times b

62
00:02:49.947 --> 00:02:52.580
equals r prime minus r.

63
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.506
Both remainders lie in the

64
00:02:55.605 --> 00:02:58.081
window from zero up to b

65
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.251
so their difference is strictly

66
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.529
between minus b and b.

67
00:03:03.529 --> 00:03:07.591
The only multiple of b in there is zero.

68
00:03:07.591 --> 00:03:11.850
So r equals r prime, and q equals q prime.

69
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.094
That is the pattern for exactly one

70
00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:18.230
show that one exists, then assume two

71
00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.320
and prove they are equal.

72
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.126
Negative numbers need care.

73
00:03:23.126 --> 00:03:25.928
Divide minus twenty-six by seven.

74
00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.401
It is tempting to write minus

75
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:30.956
three times seven, minus five.

76
00:03:30.956 --> 00:03:32.852
That equation is true

77
00:03:32.852 --> 00:03:35.736
but minus five is not a remainder

78
00:03:35.736 --> 00:03:37.220
it is below zero.

79
00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.748
Go one more jump down

80
00:03:39.748 --> 00:03:42.025
to minus twenty-eight.

81
00:03:42.124 --> 00:03:44.500
Then minus twenty-six is

82
00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:47.865
minus four times seven, plus two.

83
00:03:47.865 --> 00:03:51.330
And two is between zero and seven.

84
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.545
Python's divmod gives minus four and two.

85
00:03:55.545 --> 00:03:59.032
Languages like C round towards zero

86
00:03:59.032 --> 00:04:02.520
and give minus three and minus five.

87
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.668
Back to the board.

88
00:04:04.668 --> 00:04:07.979
Here are the divisors of eighty-four

89
00:04:07.979 --> 00:04:10.419
and the divisors of thirty.

90
00:04:10.419 --> 00:04:14.340
They have one, two, three and six in common.

91
00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.105
The greatest common divisor of a and b

92
00:04:18.195 --> 00:04:21.699
is a common divisor that is at least as

93
00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:24.844
big as every other common divisor.

94
00:04:24.844 --> 00:04:26.282
Here it is six.

95
00:04:26.282 --> 00:04:29.517
Squares of side six tile the board

96
00:04:29.517 --> 00:04:32.122
fourteen along, five across

97
00:04:32.122 --> 00:04:33.650
seventy squares.

98
00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.924
One case has no gcd at all: zero and zero.

99
00:04:37.924 --> 00:04:40.387
Every integer divides zero

100
00:04:40.387 --> 00:04:43.026
because zero is d times zero.

101
00:04:43.114 --> 00:04:46.368
So zero and zero have common divisors

102
00:04:46.456 --> 00:04:50.150
as large as you like, and no greatest one.

103
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.164
If a is not zero, every common divisor lies

104
00:04:54.249 --> 00:04:56.123
between minus a and a.

105
00:04:56.123 --> 00:04:58.679
There are only finitely many

106
00:04:58.679 --> 00:05:00.724
so the greatest exists.

107
00:05:00.724 --> 00:05:04.302
That is why the definition says: a and b

108
00:05:04.302 --> 00:05:05.580
not both zero.

109
00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.860
Listing divisors is one method.

110
00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.340
A second method

111
00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.214
factor both numbers into primes.

112
00:05:13.214 --> 00:05:16.785
Eighty-four is two squared, times three

113
00:05:16.785 --> 00:05:17.917
times seven.

114
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:21.400
Thirty is two, times three, times five.

115
00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.989
Take each prime they share

116
00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:27.638
with the smaller exponent: two times three.

117
00:05:27.638 --> 00:05:28.550
Six again.

118
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.558
Both methods are slow for big numbers.

119
00:05:31.558 --> 00:05:34.217
Take ten thousand four hundred three

120
00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:37.225
and nine thousand nine hundred ninety-one.

121
00:05:37.295 --> 00:05:40.513
Trial division tries about one hundred numbers

122
00:05:40.582 --> 00:05:42.891
before it finds the common prime

123
00:05:42.891 --> 00:05:44.220
one hundred three.

124
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.295
So: dividing a by a positive b gives exactly

125
00:05:49.397 --> 00:05:52.563
one quotient and one remainder

126
00:05:52.563 --> 00:05:56.241
with the remainder from zero up to

127
00:05:56.241 --> 00:05:58.488
but not including, b.

128
00:05:58.488 --> 00:06:02.880
And the gcd is the largest common divisor.

129
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:04.718
Try it yourself

130
00:06:04.718 --> 00:06:07.415
divide one hundred by seven

131
00:06:07.415 --> 00:06:10.390
and minus one hundred by seven.

132
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.740
In part two, a fast way to the gcd

133
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.083
cut the largest squares off the board

134
00:06:17.083 --> 00:06:18.540
again and again.
